+### JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a merge patch and returns the merged document:
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const target = { title: 'Goodbye!', author: { givenName: 'John', familyName: 'Doe' } };
+
+applyMergePatch(target, {
+  title: 'Hello!',
+  author: { familyName: null },
+}); // { title: 'Hello!', author: { givenName: 'John' } }
+```
+
+A merge patch is not a list of operations but a document shaped like the one it updates, which makes
+it a smaller thing to write than a patch and a blunter one: it describes the result rather than the
+change. As with `applyPatch`, the call leaves `target` and `patch` as they were, and the result
+shares no object or array with either of them.
+
+It throws a `JsonMergePatchError` when the patch is not a well-formed merge patch document
+(`error.code` is `'invalid-patch'`), which, since any JSON value is a merge patch, means a patch that
+is not a JSON value: as for the `value` of a patch operation, `undefined`, `NaN`, an infinity, a
+function, a symbol, a bigint, a hole in an array, an object of another kind such as a `Date` or a
+`Map`, and a value that contains itself are rejected, and the error message names the place inside
+the patch. Applying a well-formed merge patch cannot fail: every merge patch describes a change to
+every document, so there is no location to fail to find and no condition to test. Notable points of
+the specification:
+
+- An object patch is merged into an object target member by member, all the way down: a member the
+  patch gives takes the patch's value, a member the patch does not mention is left alone, and a
+  member the patch sets to `null` is removed. Removing a member the target does not have is not an
+  error, it changes nothing.
+- Anything else — an array, a string, a number, a boolean, `null` — replaces what it is applied to,
+  and so does an object patch whose target is not an object. There is therefore no merge patch that
+  sets a member to `null`, and none that changes a single element of an array: a new array is sent
+  whole. Use JSON Patch where such a change has to be expressed.
+- A `null` the target holds is a value like any other and is kept; it is only in the patch that
+  `null` means removal.
+- As in JSON Patch, only a document's own members are reachable: `__proto__` in a patch or a target
+  is the member of that name, never the prototype of anything.
+
+`MergePatch`, the type of a patch, is `JsonValue` under another name, so a patch needs no cast; a
+`target` whose shape is only known at runtime does, as elsewhere in the library.
+
